{"surah": 82, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "إِذَا ٱلسَّمَآءُ ٱنفَطَرَتْ ", "translation": "When the sky breaks apart", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱنفَطَرَتْ", "translit": "ْtَrَṭَfnٱ", "root": "فطر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "وَإِذَا ٱلْكَوَاكِبُ ٱنتَثَرَتْ ", "translation": "And when the stars fall, scattering,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْكَوَاكِبُ", "translit": "ُbِkāَwَkْlٱ", "root": "كوكب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱنتَثَرَتْ", "translit": "ْtَrَhtَtnٱ", "root": "نثر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "وَإِذَا ٱلْبِحَارُ فُجِّرَتْ ", "translation": "And when the seas are erupted", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْبِحَارُ", "translit": "ُrāَḥِbْlٱ", "root": "بحر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فُجِّرَتْ", "translit": "ْtَrِّjgُf", "root": "فجر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "وَإِذَا ٱلْقُبُورُ بُعْثِرَتْ ", "translation": "And when the [contents of] graves are scattered [i.e., exposed],", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْقُبُورُ", "translit": "ُrwُbُqْlٱ", "root": "قبر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بُعْثِرَتْ", "translit": "ْtَrِhtْʿعُb", "root": "بعثر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "عَلِمَتْ نَفْسٌ مَّا قَدَّمَتْ وَأَخَّرَتْ ", "translation": "A soul will [then] know what it has put forth and kept back.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "عَلِمَتْ", "translit": "ْtَmِlَʿع", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نَفْسٌ", "translit": "ٌsْfَn", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَّا", "translit": "āَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "قَدَّمَتْ", "translit": "ْtَmَّdَq", "root": "قدم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَأَخَّرَتْ", "translit": "ْtَrَّhkَaʾَw", "root": "اخر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ مَا غَرَّكَ بِرَبِّكَ ٱلْكَرِيمِ ", "translation": "O mankind, what has deceived you concerning your Lord, the Generous,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "غَرَّكَ", "translit": "َkَّrَغ", "root": "غرر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِرَبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَrِb", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْكَرِيمِ", "translit": "ِmyِrَkْlٱ", "root": "كرم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَكَ فَسَوَّىٰكَ فَعَدَلَكَ ", "translation": "Who created you, proportioned you, and balanced you?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "خَلَقَكَ", "translit": "َkَqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَسَوَّىٰكَ", "translit": "َkٰàَّwَsَf", "root": "سوي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَعَدَلَكَ", "translit": "َkَlَdَʿعَf", "root": "عدل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "فِىٓ أَىِّ صُورَةٍ مَّا شَآءَ رَكَّبَكَ ", "translation": "In whatever form He willed has He assembled you.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَىِّ", "translit": "ِّàَaʾ", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "صُورَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَrwُṣ", "root": "صور"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَّا", "translit": "āَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "شَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "رَكَّبَكَ", "translit": "َkَbَّkَr", "root": "ركب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 9, "text": "كَلَّا بَلْ تُكَذِّبُونَ بِٱلدِّينِ ", "translation": "No! But you deny the Recompense.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "كَلَّا", "translit": "āَّlَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تُكَذِّبُونَ", "translit": "َnwُbِّhdَkُt", "root": "كذب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِٱلدِّينِ", "translit": "ِnyِّdlٱِb", "root": "دين"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 10, "text": "وَإِنَّ عَلَيْكُمْ لَحَٰفِظِينَ ", "translation": "And indeed, [appointed] over you are keepers,[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَلَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَحَٰفِظِينَ", "translit": "َnyِẓِfَٰḥَl", "root": "حفظ"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 11, "text": "كِرَامًا كَٰتِبِينَ ", "translation": "Noble and recording;", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "كِرَامًا", "translit": "āًmāَrِk", "root": "كرم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَٰتِبِينَ", "translit": "َnyِbِtَٰk", "root": "كتب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 12, "text": "يَعْلَمُونَ مَا تَفْعَلُونَ ", "translation": "They know whatever you do.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmَlْʿعَy", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَفْعَلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَʿعْfَt", "root": "فعل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 13, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلْأَبْرَارَ لَفِى نَعِيمٍ ", "translation": "Indeed, the righteous will be in pleasure,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْأَبْرَارَ", "translit": "َrāَrْbَaʾْlٱ", "root": "برر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَفِى", "translit": "àِfَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "نَعِيمٍ", "translit": "ٍmyِʿعَn", "root": "نعم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 14, "text": "وَإِنَّ ٱلْفُجَّارَ لَفِى جَحِيمٍ ", "translation": "And indeed, the wicked will be in Hellfire.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْفُجَّارَ", "translit": "َrāَّjgُfْlٱ", "root": "فجر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَفِى", "translit": "àِfَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "جَحِيمٍ", "translit": "ٍmyِḥَjg", "root": "جحم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 15, "text": "يَصْلَوْنَهَا يَوْمَ ٱلدِّينِ ", "translation": "They will [enter to] burn therein on the Day of Recompense,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَصْلَوْنَهَا", "translit": "āَhَnْwَlْṣَy", "root": "صلي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلدِّينِ", "translit": "ِnyِّdlٱ", "root": "دين"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 16, "text": "وَمَا هُمْ عَنْهَا بِغَآئِبِينَ ", "translation": "And never therefrom will they be absent.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَنْهَا", "translit": "āَhْnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِغَآئِبِينَ", "translit": "َnyِbِyʾٓāَغِb", "root": "غيب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 17, "text": "وَمَآ أَدْرَىٰكَ مَا يَوْمُ ٱلدِّينِ ", "translation": "And what can make you know what is the Day of Recompense?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَدْرَىٰكَ", "translit": "َkٰàَrْdَaʾ", "root": "دري"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَوْمُ", "translit": "ُmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلدِّينِ", "translit": "ِnyِّdlٱ", "root": "دين"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 18, "text": "ثُمَّ مَآ أَدْرَىٰكَ مَا يَوْمُ ٱلدِّينِ ", "translation": "Then, what can make you know what is the Day of Recompense?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَآ", "translit": "ٓāَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَدْرَىٰكَ", "translit": "َkٰàَrْdَaʾ", "root": "دري"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَوْمُ", "translit": "ُmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلدِّينِ", "translit": "ِnyِّdlٱ", "root": "دين"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 19, "text": "يَوْمَ لَا تَمْلِكُ نَفْسٌ لِّنَفْسٍ شَيْـًٔاۖ وَٱلْأَمْرُ يَوْمَئِذٍ لِّلَّهِ ", "translation": "It is the Day when a soul will not possess for another soul [power to do] a thing; and the command, that Day, is [entirely] with Allāh.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَمْلِكُ", "translit": "ُkِlْmَt", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "نَفْسٌ", "translit": "ٌsْfَn", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لِّنَفْسٍ", "translit": "ٍsْfَnِّl", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "شَيْـًٔا", "translit": "āًٔـْyَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَمْرُ", "translit": "ُrْmَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَوْمَئِذٍ", "translit": "ٍhdِyʾَmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لِّلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِّl", "root": "اله"}], "hasIhya": true}], "tafsir": {"6": [{"arabic": "فيا أيها الإنسان ما غرك بربك الكريم حيث أغلقت الأبواب وأرخيت الستور واستترت عن الخلائق فقارفت الفجور فماذا تفعل وقد شهدت عليك جوارحك فالويل كل الويل لنا معشر الغافلين يرسل الله لنا سيد المرسلين وينزل عليه الكتاب المبين ويخبرنا بهذه الصفات من نعوت يوم الدين ثم يعرفنا غفلتنا ويقول اقترب الناس حسابهم وهم في غفلة معرضون ما يأتيهم من ذكر من ربهم محدث إلا استمعوه وهم يلعبون لاهية قلوبهم ثم يعرفنا قرب القيامة فيقول اقتربت الساعة وانشق القمر إنهم يرونه بعيدا ونراه قريبا وما يدريك لعل السا", "english": "So prepare yourself, O wretched one, for this Day of immense magnitude, of prolonged duration, of overwhelming dominion, whose appointed time is near — a Day when you shall see the heaven split asunder, and the stars scattered in its terror, and the shining planets extinguished, and the sun folded up, and the mountains set in motion, and the pregnant camels neglected, and the wild beasts gathered together, and the seas set ablaze, and the souls paired with their bodies, and the Hellfire ignited, and Paradise brought near, and the mountains pulverized, and the earth stretched out — a Day when you shall see the earth shaken with its quaking, and the earth casts forth its burdens. On that Day shall people issue forth in scattered groups to be shown their deeds — a Day when the earth and the mountains are lifted and crushed with a single crushing. Then on that Day the Imminent Event shall come to pass, and the heaven shall split open, for on that Day it shall be frail, and the angels shall be at its edges, and eight shall bear the Throne of your Lord above them on that Day. On that Day you shall be presented — no secret of yours shall remain hidden. A Day when the mountains shall move and you shall see the earth laid bare — a Day when the earth shall be shaken with a violent shaking, and the mountains crumbled to dust, becoming scattered particles — a Day when people shall be like scattered moths, and the mountains like carded wool — a Day when every nursing mother shall forget what she nursed, and every pregnant female shall cast down her burden, and you shall see people drunk, yet they are not drunk, but the chastisement of God is severe — a Day when the earth shall be changed to other than this earth, and the heavens, and they shall appear before God, the One, the All-Compelling — a Day when the mountains shall be blown away as dust, leaving a level, bare plain, wherein you see no crookedness nor curve — a Day when you see the mountains and suppose them solid, yet they pass by like the passing of clouds — a Day when the heaven shall split open and become rosy like molten oil. Then on that Day no human nor jinn shall be asked about his sin. A Day when the disobedient shall be prevented from speech, and shall not be asked about their crimes, but shall be seized by their forelocks and their feet — a Day when every soul shall find what it did of good brought forth present, and what it did of evil, wishing that between it and that evil there were a far distance — a Day when every soul shall know what it has sent ahead, and shall witness what it has forwarded and what it has left behind — a Day when tongues shall be struck mute and the bodily members shall speak — a Day whose mention turned the hair of the Master of the Messengers white, when al-Ṣiddīq (may God be pleased with him) said to him: \"I see that you have turned white-haired, O Messenger of God.\" He said: \"Sūrat Hūd and its sisters have made me white-haired\" — namely, al-Wāqiʿah, al-Mursalāt, ʿAmma Yatasāʾalūn, and Idhā al-Shamsu Kuwwirat.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1339", "section_title_en": "The Names and Attributes of the Day of Resurrection — An Exhortation to the Negligent", "section_index": 1339, "anchors": "- Root: يوم (yawm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"اليوم: وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها، وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — the day as a temporal span, extended metaphorically to any epoch or era.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — theological usage denotes the appointed time of reckoning, magnified by divine attribution (أيام الله).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): a measured solar period. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ذكر في أيام العرب كذا أي في وقائعها\" — days as epochal events; thus \"the Day\" is the supreme epochal event of divine justice.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: النفس: الروح والذات — the self, soul, or very essence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح... وقوله: ويحذركم الله نفسه فنفسه: ذاته\" — nafs as the innermost reality of a being; in the eschatological context, souls are \"paired\" (زوجت) with bodies for judgment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the soul as life-principle. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — nafs as the total self, accountable in its entirety.\n- Root: ارض (arḍ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء — the earth as the corporeal substratum beneath the heavens.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء... ويعبر بها عن أسفل الشيء\" — the earth as the lowest register of creation, subject to total transformation on that Day.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical earth. Metaphorical: \"تأرض فلان: لزم الأرض\" — to cling low; on that Day the earth itself is leveled, flattened, and replaced.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The repeated \"يوم\" clauses function as temporal restrictors (ẓurūf) binding each predicate to that single, all-encompassing moment; the accumulation of names (أسامي) serves not as mere repetition but as focused predication upon the multifarious realities of a single event.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}], "10": [{"arabic": "بمعروف أو نهيا عن منكر أو أن تنطق بحاجتك في معيشتك التي لا بد لك منها أتنكرون أن عليكم حافظين كراما كاتبين عن اليمين وعن الشمال قعيد ما يلفظ من قول إلا لديه رقيب عتيد أما يستحي أحدكم إذا نشرت صحيفته التي أملاها صدر نهاره كان أكثر ما فيها ليس من أمر دينه ولا دنياه وعن بعض الصحابة قال إن الرجل ليكلمني بالكلام لجوابه أشهى إلي من الماء البارد إلى الظمآن فأترك جوابه خيفة أن يكون فضولا وقال مطرف ليعظم جلال الله في قلوبكم فلا تذكروه عند مثل قول أحدكم للكلب والحمار اللهم اخزه وما أشبه ذلك واعلم أن فضول الكلام لا ينحصر بل المهم محصور في كتاب الله تع", "english": "ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ said: \"Those before you used to detest excess of speech, and they counted as excess of speech everything beyond the Book of God, Mighty and Majestic, and the Sunnah of His Messenger, peace and blessings be upon him—or a command to what is right, or a prohibition of what is wrong, or your uttering your need concerning your livelihood which you cannot do without. Do you deny that {«عَلَيْكُمْ حَافِظِينَ كِرَامًا كَاتِبِينَ، يَعْلَمُونَ مَا تَفْعَلُونَ»} (over you are watchers, noble scribes, knowing what you do)? {«مَا يَلْفِظُ مِنْ قَوْلٍ إِلَّا لَدَيْهِ رَقِيبٌ عَتِيدٌ»} (He utters not a word but that there is with him a watcher, ready.) Is not one of you ashamed that when his scroll is unfolded—which he dictated through the early part of his day—most of what is in it pertains neither to his religion nor to his worldly affairs?\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 660", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Etiquette of Speech — Section 660: The Second Affliction — Excess of Speech (Faḍl al-Kalām)", "section_index": 660, "anchors": "- Root: فضل (Faḍl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Excess, surplus, that which remains beyond need; the superfluous portion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفضل: الزيادة عن الاقتصاد\" — increase beyond moderation; blameworthy when it exceeds the due measure, as in speech surpassing the need.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"مال فاضل: كثير يفضل عن القوت\" — wealth exceeding sustenance. Here applied to speech: that which exceeds the necessary portion is \"faḍl\" (excess), blameworthy by analogy.\n- Root: ابي (Abā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Refusal, strong aversion, stubborn rejection.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإباء: شدة الامتناع\" — intense refusal; not every refusal is abā', but abā' is the emphatic form.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"أبى الله إلا أن يكون كذا\" — God refuses but that a thing be so; used of divine will overriding human inclination.\n- Root: حدث (Ḥadath)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: New occurrence, event; that which comes to be after not being.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحدوث: كون الشيء بعد أن لم يكن\" — origination after non-existence; al-muḥdath: that which is originated.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"نزلت به حوادث الدهر\" — calamities of time befall one. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"كان عمر محدثا\" — one whose inner discernment is inspired, as if spoken to.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين العامل والمعمول (Interposition between Operator and Operand) — The periodic structure here interposes explanatory clauses between the governing verb and its object, as in \"واعلم أن فضول الكلام لا ينحصر بل المهم محصور\" — the scope of the first clause is restricted by the exceptive particle \"بل\" (nay), requiring the reader to hold the initial assertion in suspension until the qualification arrives.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "13": [{"arabic": "تعالى حيث قال {إن الأبرار لفي نعيم وإن الفجار لفي جحيم} قال فأين رحمة الله قال قريب من المحسنين ثم قال سليمان يا أبا حازم أي عباد الله أكرم قال أهل البر والتقوى قال فأي الأعمال أفضل قال أداء الفرائض مع اجتناب المحارم قال فأي الكلام أسمع قال قول الحق عند من تخاف وترجوا قال فأي المؤمنين أكيس قال رجل عمل بطاعة الله ودعا الناس إليها قال فأي المؤمنين أخسر قال رجل خطا في هوى أخيه وهو ظالم فباع آخرته بدنيا غيره قال سليمان ما تقول فيما نحن فيه قال أو تعفيني قال لا بد فإنها نصيحة تلقيها إلي قال يا أمير المؤم", "english": "...my need, so I said: \"I have only been placed in this station by the swords of the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār, while their sons are dying of hunger. So fear God, and deliver to them their rights.\" He lowered his head, then raised it and said: \"Present your need to us.\" I said: \"ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb—may God be pleased with him—performed the Pilgrimage.\" He said to his treasurer: \"How much did he spend?\" He replied: \"A little over ten dirhams.\" And I see here wealth that the necks of camels cannot bear to carry. Then he left. Thus were their manner of entering upon the sultans when they were compelled [to do so], and they would risk their very souls to exact vengeance for God against their oppressors.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 392", "section_title_en": "Section 392 — The Conduct of the Religious Scholars with Rulers, and the States that Arise from Mixing with Them", "section_index": 392, "anchors": "- Root: قول (q-w-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Speech, utterance, saying; the act of articulating words with meaning.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological discourse; the content of what is said as binding or informative.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: articulated speech. Metaphorical: a statement of truth or counsel delivered with authority.\n- Root: ملك (m-l-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Possession, dominion, sovereignty; to have power over something.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The king (malik) is one who disposes by command and prohibition over a people; kingship (mulk) is of two kinds: actual possession/authority and the capacity for it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: ownership and rule. Metaphorical: mastery over oneself, or the seizure of control over affairs.\n- Root: ولي (w-l-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Nearness, closeness; guardianship, governance; to be in charge of a matter.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The walī is the one who undertakes a matter; wilāyah is the office of governance or authority over others.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: proximity and friendship. Metaphorical: the assumption of authority (tawallī) over a public office.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Focused Predication (باب الحصر والتقييد) — Constructions such as \"إنما\" and \"ما ... إلا\" restrict the predicate to a single referent, emphasizing exclusivity in the assertion.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}], "19": [{"arabic": "لا يغنى مولى عن مولى شيئا ويوم لا تملك نفس لنفس شيئا ويوم يدعون إلى نار جهنم دعا ويوم يسحبون فى النار على وجوههم ويوم تقلب وجوههم فى النار ويوم لا يجزى والد عن ولده ويوم يفر المرء من أخيه وأمه وأبيه ويوم لا ينطقون ولا يؤذن لهم فيعتذرون يوم لا مرد له من الله يوم هم بارزون ويوم هم على النار يفتنون يوم لا ينفع مال ولا بنون يوم لا ينفع الظالمين معذرتهم ولهم اللعنة ولهم سوء الدار يوم ترد فيه المعاذير وتبلى السرائر وتظهر الضمائر وتكشف الأستار يوم تخشع فيه الأبصار وتسكن الأصوات ويقل فيه الالتفات وتبرز", "english": "So prepare yourself, O wretched one, for this Day of immense magnitude, of prolonged duration, of overwhelming dominion, whose appointed time is near — a Day when you shall see the heaven split asunder, and the stars scattered in its terror, and the shining planets extinguished, and the sun folded up, and the mountains set in motion, and the pregnant camels neglected, and the wild beasts gathered together, and the seas set ablaze, and the souls paired with their bodies, and the Hellfire ignited, and Paradise brought near, and the mountains pulverized, and the earth stretched out — a Day when you shall see the earth shaken with its quaking, and the earth casts forth its burdens. On that Day shall people issue forth in scattered groups to be shown their deeds — a Day when the earth and the mountains are lifted and crushed with a single crushing. Then on that Day the Imminent Event shall come to pass, and the heaven shall split open, for on that Day it shall be frail, and the angels shall be at its edges, and eight shall bear the Throne of your Lord above them on that Day. On that Day you shall be presented — no secret of yours shall remain hidden. A Day when the mountains shall move and you shall see the earth laid bare — a Day when the earth shall be shaken with a violent shaking, and the mountains crumbled to dust, becoming scattered particles — a Day when people shall be like scattered moths, and the mountains like carded wool — a Day when every nursing mother shall forget what she nursed, and every pregnant female shall cast down her burden, and you shall see people drunk, yet they are not drunk, but the chastisement of God is severe — a Day when the earth shall be changed to other than this earth, and the heavens, and they shall appear before God, the One, the All-Compelling — a Day when the mountains shall be blown away as dust, leaving a level, bare plain, wherein you see no crookedness nor curve — a Day when you see the mountains and suppose them solid, yet they pass by like the passing of clouds — a Day when the heaven shall split open and become rosy like molten oil. Then on that Day no human nor jinn shall be asked about his sin. A Day when the disobedient shall be prevented from speech, and shall not be asked about their crimes, but shall be seized by their forelocks and their feet — a Day when every soul shall find what it did of good brought forth present, and what it did of evil, wishing that between it and that evil there were a far distance — a Day when every soul shall know what it has sent ahead, and shall witness what it has forwarded and what it has left behind — a Day when tongues shall be struck mute and the bodily members shall speak — a Day whose mention turned the hair of the Master of the Messengers white, when al-Ṣiddīq (may God be pleased with him) said to him: \"I see that you have turned white-haired, O Messenger of God.\" He said: \"Sūrat Hūd and its sisters have made me white-haired\" — namely, al-Wāqiʿah, al-Mursalāt, ʿAmma Yatasāʾalūn, and Idhā al-Shamsu Kuwwirat.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1339", "section_title_en": "The Names and Attributes of the Day of Resurrection — An Exhortation to the Negligent", "section_index": 1339, "anchors": "- Root: يوم (yawm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"اليوم: وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها، وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — the day as a temporal span, extended metaphorically to any epoch or era.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — theological usage denotes the appointed time of reckoning, magnified by divine attribution (أيام الله).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): a measured solar period. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ذكر في أيام العرب كذا أي في وقائعها\" — days as epochal events; thus \"the Day\" is the supreme epochal event of divine justice.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: النفس: الروح والذات — the self, soul, or very essence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح... وقوله: ويحذركم الله نفسه فنفسه: ذاته\" — nafs as the innermost reality of a being; in the eschatological context, souls are \"paired\" (زوجت) with bodies for judgment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the soul as life-principle. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — nafs as the total self, accountable in its entirety.\n- Root: ارض (arḍ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء — the earth as the corporeal substratum beneath the heavens.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء... ويعبر بها عن أسفل الشيء\" — the earth as the lowest register of creation, subject to total transformation on that Day.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical earth. Metaphorical: \"تأرض فلان: لزم الأرض\" — to cling low; on that Day the earth itself is leveled, flattened, and replaced.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The repeated \"يوم\" clauses function as temporal restrictors (ẓurūf) binding each predicate to that single, all-encompassing moment; the accumulation of names (أسامي) serves not as mere repetition but as focused predication upon the multifarious realities of a single event.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}]}, "wordTiming": {"minshawi": {"1": [[1, 370, 1428], [2, 1428, 4215], [3, 4215, 5400]], "82:1": [[1, 370, 1428], [2, 1428, 4215], [3, 4215, 5400]], "2": [[1, 30, 1550], [2, 1550, 3491], [3, 3491, 4610]], "82:2": [[1, 30, 1550], [2, 1550, 3491], [3, 3491, 4610]], "3": [[1, 10, 1525], [2, 1525, 2988], [3, 2988, 3960]], "82:3": [[1, 10, 1525], [2, 1525, 2988], [3, 2988, 3960]], "4": [[1, 260, 1612], [2, 1612, 2882], [3, 2882, 3860]], "82:4": [[1, 260, 1612], [2, 1612, 2882], [3, 2882, 3860]], "5": [[1, 380, 1488], [2, 1488, 2296], [3, 2296, 3574], [4, 3574, 4865], [5, 4865, 6850]], "82:5": [[1, 380, 1488], [2, 1488, 2296], [3, 2296, 3574], [4, 3574, 4865], [5, 4865, 6850]], "6": [[1, 460, 4166], [2, 4166, 5660], [